// 3.1 POLYFIELD — ✅ FIXED 2026-08-30. Was a KNOWN GAP that printed a wrong number; now asserts.
// Correct output: poly 1.5 7
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG. Deriving a parameter type from call sites is only valid when EVERY call site is
// visible. Referencing a function by name lowers to a make_closure over a `__fnref_<target>`
// trampoline, and that closure can be invoked from anywhere — a call the analysis never sees.
// `poly` was specialized to CPt on the strength of its ONE visible direct call, so the field read
// and the return type were both labelled float; the closure path then read QPt's INT field through
// that float label (printing 7 as 3.45845952088873e-323, the integer's bit pattern as a double).
//
// THE FIX — three parts, all keyed off the ADDRESS-TAKEN set (b.ir_tramps):
//   1. ESCAPE ANALYSIS: a struct passed to an address-taken function stays on the HEAP. This is
//      what unblocked everything — nova_field_get_or was ALREADY correct (it reads slot 0 as the
//      type hash and BOXES floats and bools), but its guard is nova_mem_find_tag == NOVA_MEM_STRUCT,
//      so a SROA'd STACK struct was rejected. That, and only that, defeated the third attempt.
//   2. RETURN TYPE: ir_analyze_return_type / ir_returns_raw_double no longer seed params from fpt
//      for an address-taken function, so frt stops publishing a float return derived from calls we
//      cannot see.
//   3. FIELD READ: inside an address-taken function the field read resolves BY NAME. Measured, not
//      assumed — the read arrives with typ=FLOAT (lowering had already specialized), so gating on
//      typ=="any" never fired. Inside such a function EVERY compile-time field type is a guess.
//
// The cost is confined to address-taken functions reading un-inferrable fields. Statically typed
// code keeps the inline GEP+load and full SROA, so GATE 4/5 are untouched.
//
// PREVIOUSLY-FAILED APPROACHES, kept so they are not re-attempted:
//   a. Exclude address-taken fns from specialization ALONE — closure call right, DIRECT call wrong
//      (printed 4609434218613702656, the bits of 1.5 as an integer). Reproduced during this fix:
//      it is parts 1+2 without part 3.
//   b. Route un-inferrable field reads via nova_rt_field_get at LOWERING time — right mechanism,
//      wrong time: the param fixpoint has not run, so every unannotated struct param looks
//      un-inferrable and ordinary code fell from fmul/fadd to a runtime name walk.
//   c. Same routing after the fixpoint — correct placement, but structs were still SROA'd onto the
//      stack, so field_get's tag guard returned null. Part 1 is what makes (c) work.

type CPt
    cx: float
    cy: float

type QPt
    cx: int
    cy: int

fn poly(v)
    v.cx

fn apply_it(f, v)
    f(v)

fn main()
    let p = CPt { cx: 1.5, cy: 2.5 }
    let r = QPt { cx: 7, cy: 8 }

    // The DIRECT call: a float field, read inside an address-taken function.
    let direct = poly(p)
    assert_eq(str(direct), "1.5")

    // The CLOSURE call: the SAME function reached through the __fnref_ trampoline with a struct
    // whose `cx` is an INT. This is the call the analysis cannot see, and the one that was wrong.
    let via_closure = apply_it(poly, r)
    assert_eq(str(via_closure), "7")

    print("poly " + str(direct) + " " + str(via_closure))
    print("_f31_polyfield passed")
